BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-d1d5", "after": "sec-intro-p1", "title": "The D1-D5 system and AdS3/CFT2", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Take type IIB string theory on $\mathbb R^{1,5}\times T^4$ and stack $Q_5$ D5-branes wrapping the whole $T^4$ and $Q_1$ D1-branes, all extended along one common spatial direction. At low energies the open strings on this bound state, the D1-D5 system, give a two-dimensional field theory on the common direction, which flows to a conformal field theory with $\mathcal N=(4,4)$ supersymmetry. Its central charge is about $6Q_1Q_5$. The branes also curve spacetime, and near them the geometry becomes $AdS_3\times S^3\times T^4$, with flux through $AdS_3$ and $S^3$. The AdS/CFT correspondence says that string theory in this throat equals the CFT. Brown and Henneaux showed that any gravity on $AdS_3$ has a boundary Virasoro symmetry with $c=3R/2G_3$, which matches the CFT's central charge. S-duality turns D1- and D5-branes into fundamental strings and NS5-branes, giving the same geometry with NS-NS flux instead. The paper uses this second frame because strings there have a tractable worldsheet.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-symorb", "after": "sec-intro-p4", "title": "Symmetric orbifold CFTs", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>Given any CFT $\mathcal M$, take $N$ copies and gauge the permutation group $S_N$. The result, the symmetric orbifold $\mathcal M^N/S_N$, has central charge $Nc$. Like all orbifold CFTs, symmetric orbifold CFTs have twisted sectors: fields may come back to a permuted copy when they go around a point. Twisted sectors are labelled by conjugacy classes of $S_N$, that is by cycle shapes. In a sector with a single cycle of length $w$, the $w$ copies join into one copy living on a circle $w$ times longer, so its excitations have energies in units of $1/w$. The lowest such operator, the twist field, has weight $\frac{c}{24}(w-1/w)$. Correlators of twist fields are computed by lifting to a covering surface on which all the copies become one, as shown by Lunin and Mathur. At large $N$ these correlators have a $1/N$ expansion organised by the genus of the cover, much like the 't Hooft expansion of a gauge theory. The paper identifies these covers with string worldsheets.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-cs", "after": "sec-dual_cft-p5", "title": "Chern–Simons fields on AdS3 and boundary currents", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>In three dimensions a gauge field can have a Chern–Simons action, $\frac{k}{4\pi}\int A\wedge dA$, or for two $U(1)$ fields $\frac{k}{4\pi}\int A\wedge dB$. Such a theory has no local propagating degrees of freedom: its equations of motion set the field strength to zero. On a space with a boundary, however, gauge transformations that do not vanish at the boundary act on physical states. To have a good variational principle one fixes one component of the gauge field at the boundary, say $A_{\bar z}$. The other component then becomes a holomorphic current of the boundary theory, and $k$ becomes the level of its current algebra. Changing which component is fixed changes the boundary theory by a current-current deformation. In $AdS_3$ the Chern–Simons term dominates the Maxwell term at long distances, so bulk $U(1)$ gauge fields are of this kind. In the paper the CFT's $8+8$ currents, of levels $Q_1$ and $Q_5$, come from such bulk fields, and the decoupled $\hat T^4$ turns out to be a level-1 sector made entirely of boundary modes.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ads3-ws", "after": "sec-string_ws-p2", "title": "Strings on AdS3 with NS-NS flux", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>With only NS-NS flux the string worldsheet is an exactly solvable CFT, a Wess–Zumino–Witten (WZW) model. A WZW model is a sigma model on a group manifold together with a $B$-field whose field strength is proportional to the volume form; the integer level $k$ fixes both the radius, $R^2=k\alpha'$, and the flux. $S^3$ is the group $SU(2)$ and $AdS_3$ is $SL(2,\mathbb R)$, both at level $Q_5$. The unitary representations of $SL(2,\mathbb R)$ include discrete ones, which describe strings bound in the interior, and continuous ones. Maldacena and Ooguri showed that the full spectrum also contains spectrally flowed sectors, strings winding $w$ times near the boundary. The continuous representations there are long strings, which reach the boundary at finite energy. At $k=1$ the RNS description fails, and the hybrid formalism describes a string whose worldsheet sits at the boundary. The next paragraph relates the long-string continuum to the CFT's singularities.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-narain", "after": "sec-diffrent_back-p2", "title": "Toroidal CFTs and current-current deformations", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>A sigma model on a torus $T^d$ with constant metric $G$ and $B$-field is a toroidal CFT, a free theory. Each circle carries an integer momentum $p_i$ and winding $w^i$. The model has $d$ left-moving and $d$ right-moving $U(1)$ currents, so $U(1)^d\times U(1)^d$, and their charges are combinations of $p$ and $w$. The dimensions $L_0$ and $\bar L_0$ of a state are the squares of its left and right charges, plus oscillator contributions; the charge part alone is the Sugawara energy. The parameters $E=G+B$ can be changed continuously by adding $\int J_i\bar J_j$ to the action, a current-current or $J\bar J$ deformation. It is exactly marginal, and its effect on the spectrum is known exactly. The moduli space of toroidal CFTs is $O(d,d)/(O(d)\times O(d))$, modulo T-dualities in $O(d,d,\mathbb Z)$, which for instance invert the volume. In the paper the orbifold's centre of mass and the extra $\hat T^4$ are two such tori whose currents mix under $J\bar J$ deformations.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gamma0", "after": "sec-diffrent_back-p2", "title": "The modular group and its subgroup Γ0(N)", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The group $SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ of integer $2\times2$ matrices $\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ with unit determinant acts on the upper half plane by $\tau\mapsto(a\tau+b)/(c\tau+d)$. Points related by this action are equivalent, and a fundamental domain is a region containing one representative of each class, the familiar keyhole region above the unit circle. In type IIB string theory, $SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ is the S-duality group, acting on $\tau=\chi+i/g_s$, where $\chi$ is the R-R axion. Its subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$ keeps only the matrices with $c$ divisible by $N$. Fewer identifications mean a larger fundamental domain, made of $N\prod_{p|N}(1+1/p)$ copies of the $SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ domain, the product being over the primes dividing $N$. For $N=30$ this is 72 copies. In the paper $\Gamma_0(N)$ is the part of U-duality that preserves the charges $(N,1)$, so its fundamental domain is the slice of moduli space drawn in figure 1.</p>"""},
]

QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "sec-dual_cft-p1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What do $Q_1$, $Q_5$, $N$, $p$, $V$ and the grand-canonical ensemble stand for, and which regime is weakly coupled?",
     "a": r"$Q_1$ counts D1-branes (or fundamental strings) and $Q_5$ D5-branes (or NS5-branes); $N=Q_1Q_5$ sets the central charge $6N+6$. In the NS-NS frame the torus volume, $v_4$ in section 2 and $V$ later, is free, and the 10d coupling is $g_{10}^2=VQ_5/Q_1$. The grand-canonical ensemble sums CFTs over $Q_1$, weighted by $p^{Q_1}$, and large $p$ means weak coupling, $g_s^{-2}\propto p$. For $Q_5=1$ the NS-NS string is weakly coupled when $V\ll N$.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-sugra-p3", "sec-grand-p6", "section-4.1-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-why-ensemble", "at": {"node": "section-3.1-p3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does string theory describe an ensemble over $Q_1$ rather than one CFT?",
     "a": r"The NS-NS worldsheet action contains $Q_5$ but not $Q_1$. Where the spacetime central charge should contain $Q_1$, a worldsheet operator $I$ appears instead, and its correlators are not those of a constant. A coupling for $I$ can be absorbed into the string coupling and a normalisation of vertex operators. So the string has one free parameter, which was suggested to be the fugacity of an ensemble over $Q_1$.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-3.1-p3", "section-3.1-p4", "section-3.1-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-fixed-N", "at": {"node": "sec-genus_exp-p6"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Isn't the fixed-$N$ orbifold already a string theory with $g_s^2=1/N$?",
     "a": r"Only at leading order. Each genus-$g$ covering scales as $N^{1-g-n/2}$ only to leading order; its $1/N$ corrections then mix with genus-one diagrams, and redefining $g_s$ or the operators cannot separate them. Disconnected pieces also differ from products of their parts by $1+O(1/N)$, so the string's cluster decomposition fails. In the grand-canonical ensemble both problems disappear exactly.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-genus_exp-p7", "sec-genus_exp-p8", "sec-grand-p6"]},
    {"id": "q-back-to-N", "at": {"node": "sec-grand-p7"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"How does one recover correlators at a fixed $N$ from string theory?",
     "a": r"Not by reading off the coefficient of $p^N$ in a string correlator. String theory computes ratios of ensemble partition functions, so one must inverse-Laplace-transform the correlators and the vacuum diagrams separately. The operators must also be normalised by the right powers of $p$. The earlier Legendre-transform proposal agrees only at leading order. For $Q_5>1$ the paper suggests the normalisation, but the exact one needs the full string computation.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-grand-p7", "section-3.5-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-convergence", "at": {"node": "sec-grand-p2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Does the sum over $N$ converge, and are there non-perturbative corrections?",
     "a": r"In general it is not clear that the sum over $N$ converges, and string perturbation theory is not expected to; the claim is that the two asymptotic series agree. For the free orbifold on a sphere the sum does converge, for finitely many insertions. There, perturbative strings seem to capture the full partition function, with no other bulk saddles. The paper presumes this is lost away from the free orbifold, including for $Q_5>1$.",
     "answeredBy": ["footnotes", "section-3.5-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-higher-genus", "at": {"node": "sec-higher_genus_CFT-p3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does large $p$ mean strong coupling when the CFT lives on a higher-genus surface?",
     "a": r"The worldsheet is a covering of the CFT's surface, so for a surface of genus $G>1$ it has genus at least $G$. The leading vacuum diagram is then a genus-$G$ worldsheet, not a sphere, and matching powers gives $p\propto g_s^{2G-2}$. Tree-level worldsheets never contribute. The paper attributes this to the worldsheet being localised at the boundary, and does not expect it for $Q_5>1$.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-higher_genus_CFT-p2", "sec-higher_genus_CFT-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-free-knows", "at": {"node": "section-4.1-p1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"How can a free CFT know that the dual string becomes strongly coupled at large torus volume?",
     "a": r"The orbifold stays free, but its $1/N$ expansion does not. Loops around branch points permute the copies of $T^4$, and the torus zero modes along them give each genus-$g$ term a factor $V^g$. Correlators therefore expand in $V/N$, and the expansion breaks down at $V\sim N$, where the NS-NS string's 10d coupling becomes of order one.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.1-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-rr", "at": {"node": "section-4.1-p3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"For $V\gg N$ the S-dual R-R string has a small coupling. Why is it not a weakly coupled description?",
     "a": r"Its parameters come from supergravity, which fails here: the AdS radius would be far below the string scale. Then stringy modes sit at the AdS scale, and the physics is governed by Newton's constants in AdS units, which are S-duality invariant. The 10d one, $V/N$, is large. So for $Q_5=1$ the R-R picture is always strongly coupled, as is $AdS_5\times S^5$ with small flux even at $g_s\to0$.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.1-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-energy", "at": {"node": "sec-planck-p1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"At what energy does string perturbation theory break down?",
     "a": r"High-energy operators come from twisted sectors with $w$ of order the energy $E$, and the number of genus-$g$ coverings grows as $E^{3(2g-2)+2n}$. If each covering contributes an amount independent of $E$, the $1/N$ expansion fails at $E\sim N^{1/6}$. If they grow with the energy in a genus-dependent way, it may fail earlier, for instance at the naive 10d Planck scale $N^{1/8}$. The paper does not determine which.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-planck-p3", "sec-planck-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-no-vertex", "at": {"node": "sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p8"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why should the $\hat T^4$ currents have no worldsheet vertex operator?",
     "a": r"If a current had one, every single string would carry its own tower of descendants, as each string does under the NS-NS currents. But the $\hat T^4$ partition function is one overall factor of the CFT's partition function, so a multi-string state has only one such tower. The paper takes this to suggest no local vertex exists. In the bulk, the sector is a level-1 Chern–Simons theory, dual by itself to the $\hat T^4$ CFT.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p8", "sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p9"]},
    {"id": "q-dbranes", "at": {"node": "section-5.3-p5"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"States on the BPS lattice carry R-R charge. Are they D-branes?",
     "a": r"No. They decouple from all closed strings and from the coset CFT, unlike D-branes, which are worldsheet boundary conditions. The paper identifies them as pure gauge modes of bulk potentials, such as $C_3$ holonomies on $S^3$, which carry R-R charge $Q_5$ in a background with $H_3$ flux. For $Q_5>1$, non-BPS states with order-one R-R charge are expected to be genuine D-branes; for $Q_5=1$, none are expected.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-5.3-p6", "footnotes"]},
    {"id": "q-not-shown", "at": {"node": "sec-intro-p7"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What does the paper not show?",
     "a": r"The ensemble for $Q_5>1$ is only conjectured, and whether it includes non-coprime $Q_1$ is open. The deformation of the coset CFT towards other $(Q_1,Q_5)$ is known only at leading order; only the $J\bar J$ part is exact. The position of the $\theta=0$ orbifold is conjectured, and the scale of the high-energy breakdown depends on an unknown scaling. The absence of $\hat T^4$ vertex operators and D-branes is argued from the CFT, not derived on the worldsheet.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-3.5-p1", "section-5.2-p11", "sec-planck-p4"]},
]
